{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a per-feed `fetch_timeout` (integer seconds, 0 = use the global timeout). The build and the existing unit and integration tests pass; the new behaviour itself was not exercised by any test I ran (no slow-server run).\n\nWhat changed:\n- **Model**: `Feed.FetchTimeout` (`json:\"fetch_timeout\"`), `FeedModificationRequest.FetchTimeout` (`*int`), applied in `Patch`, plus a small `Feed.EffectiveFetchTimeout(global)` helper.\n- **Refresh**: `RefreshFeed` now passes the per-feed timeout to the request builder when it is above 0, otherwise the global `HTTPClientTimeout`.\n- **Storage**: new migration adds `feeds.fetch_timeout int not null default 0`; the column is included in `CreateFeed`, `UpdateFeed` and the feed query builder's select/scan.\n- **API**: `PUT /v1/feeds/{feedID}` accepts the field through the existing `updateFeedHandler` \u2192 `Patch` path, and it appears in the feed object.\n- **Client**: `fetch_timeout` added to `client.Feed` and `client.FeedModificationRequest`.\n\nThings to know:\n- Negative values are rejected in `ValidateFeedModification` with the key `error.feed_invalid_fetch_timeout`. I did not add that key to the locale files, so the error message will show the raw key until translations are added.\n- The override applies to feed refresh only. Initial feed creation still uses the global timeout, since the creation request has no `fetch_timeout` field; the task only named the feed object and the PUT endpoint.\n- There is no field for it in the web UI feed edit form; it is API-only for now.", "t": 1794462137.319}
{"tool": "_result", "name": "finish", "chars": 55, "t": 1794462137.319}
